WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:05.480
Nobody knows what the self-driving cars of
the future will look like.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.080
Once you no longer need a human in the
front seat operating a vehicle,

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.840
automakers can rethink every aspect of car
design.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.300
Since the late 1960s, however,

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.390
the federal government has enforced detailed
safety standards

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.820
governing the physical makeup of the car.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.640
Virtually every part of the car has had a
standard written about it.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.390
There's about a hundred different subjects
that have been covered

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:33.030
both in advance notices and in actual standards.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.910
The federal rule books, which have expanded
over the years,

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.829
have specifics on everything from rearview
mirror and steering wheel placement,

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:44.670
to the shape of the vehicle and the exact
placement of the seats.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.860
Since self-driving cars don't need rearview
mirrors, steering wheels,

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:49.880
or even seats necessarily,

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.820
what should the federal government do with
it's roughly 900-pages of regulations?

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.440
I will make the contention that will rattle
the cage

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.300
of some people in the auto regulation business,

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.500
that basically the entire vehicle code can
be boiled down to

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.320
be safe, and do not unfairly get in the way
of other people.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.440
Brad Templeton is an entrepreneur and software
architect,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.160
who has worked as a consultant with Google
on its self-driving car project.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.870
He says most regulation will become unnecessary
and detrimental

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:21.480
now that motor vehicles are becoming essentially
computers on wheels,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.940
meaning software, not just hardware, is essential
for safety.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:33.830
It's necessary to bring that tried, true,
trusted means of regulation to bear.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.910
John Simpson works at the California-based
nonprofit Consumer Watchdog.

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:40.450
He says that the transition to driverless
cars

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.650
doesn't mean we don't need stringent safety
standards.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:49.980
You don't say the algorithm will be written
this way, this way, this way and this way.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.540
What you do is you set a standard that says

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:59.080
the vehicle shall be able to do X under Y
circumstance.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.120
But the switch in emphasis from hardware to
software

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.890
could change the incentive structure in the
car industry

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.200
in ways that make government oversight less
necessary.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.200
One difference is that when companies discover
a safety flaw

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.300
they'll have every reason to fix the problem
immediately.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.940
With today's cars, that hasn't always been
the case.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.180
Take a classic example,

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:24.611
General Motors found a flaw in the ignition
switches and they didn't do the right thing.

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:27.200
The American public were failed by a corporate
culture

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.610
that chose to conceal rather than disclose.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.160
Replacing every ignition switch was very very
expensive

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.159
and so they were afraid of that cost.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.920
But the safety problems of the future will
primarily be bugs in software not hardware,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.159
so they'll be fixed by sending ones and zeros
over the internet

00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:47.390
without the need to return hundreds of thousands
of vehicles to the manufacturer.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:52.780
Replacing software is free, so there's no
reason to hold back on fixing something.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:56.060
Another difference is that when hardware was
all that mattered for safety,

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:01.780
regulators could easily inspect a car and
determine if it met standards.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.840
With software, scrutiny of this sort may be
impossible

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.500
because the leading self-driving car companies
are developing their platforms

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.620
through a process called machine learning.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.940
Machine learning certainly doesn't mesh in
with traditional methods of regulation

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.040
or of software development, frankly.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.310
With this method, the vehicle recognizes patterns
in its environment

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:22.870
and refines its own system through driving
experience.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:27.200
But machine learning, which is a form of AI,
is developed organically,

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.980
so humans have limited understanding of how
the system actually works.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.060
And that makes regulators nervous.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.459
Templeton fears that our desire to understand
and control the underlying system

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:41.400
could lead governments to prohibit the use
of machine learning technologies.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.800
It turns out that they do a better job, but
we don't know why,

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.019
we'll be in the situation of deliberately
deploying the thing that's worse

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:51.000
because we feel more comfortable that we understand
it.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.129
John Simpson says extreme caution is warranted

00:03:58.129 --> 00:04:02.349
because software in cars isn't like software
in other contexts.

00:04:02.349 --> 00:04:06.650
It's one thing to test Gmail in beta mode

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.790
the glitches and you lose your messages and
you say

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:12.150
'oh hell's bells I lost my messages.'

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.440
and software that when it glitches actually
might kill you.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.420
And I think that's an important distinction
to make.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.440
That these need to be perfect before we allow them
on the roads

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.390
is a mindset that has infected a lot of urban
planners

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:27.300
and a lot of, just the general pro regulation
set.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.440
Marc Scribner is a senior fellow at the free
market think tank

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:32.620
the Competitive Enterprise Institute.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:36.260
He says that its this mindset that could cost
lives on the highway.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:41.260
Delaying self driving car technology means
we are going to see additional deaths

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:44.760
that we could avoid if we simply would've allowed these
vehicles on the road

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.780
that are not perfectly safe but still safer
than the cars we have today.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:56.240
The computerization of driving also has an
impact on how we build and maintain highways.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.720
Some transportation planners say that to prepare
for self-driving cars

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:01.700
we need to construct smart infrastructure

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.980
that communicates directly with vehicles.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:07.420
Templeton says that as driving become a software-based
industry,

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:10.200
the physical highways should mimic the internet.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.460
So the internet, we like to call it the stupid
network

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:15.360
and that's 'cause the network is really really
simple.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.070
When you talk about cars you talk about stupid
roads and smart cars.

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:20.810
You don't try and make the infrastructure
smart,

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.330
you make the infrastructure as basic as possible,

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:28.320
and you put the intelligence in the cars where
it's being built by lots of competing companies

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.080
and that's where you get the piece of innovation
that shook the world that the internet had.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:37.310
If we need to install all of this new electronic
infrastructure on highways

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:39.780
and we're having problems filling potholes,

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.200
just from a pure budgetary and management
perspective, you're dreaming.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:48.680
Physical infrastructure takes decades of planning
and implementation.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.450
Templeton says self-driving cars provide an
opportunity

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:54.610
to make the roads quite a bit dumber than
they are today.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:58.940
You take what infrastructure you do need to
have and you move it in the car.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:02.520
An example is, the maps that google uses for
their car to drive

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:06.580
and they mapped all the roads so they don't
need anybody to paint new lines on the road.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:10.440
They use that virtual infrastructure much
cheaper, much more flexible,

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.400
much more ability to change your mind about
what you want.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.740
Auto safety technology, driven by the private
sector

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:23.160
is evolving much more rapidly than the federal
regulations.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.500
And some of them, right now very clearly stand
in the way

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:30.900
of consumers adopting these safer technologies.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:36.960
The more real danger is that the benefits
of this technology

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:43.330
will drive us to ignore the necessary safety
regulations right now.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:48.020
the impact that computers will have on society
is really really big here.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.330
You need a world where people are free to
come up with new innovations

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.280
that everyone else thinks they're crazy.

